# Vectors for the `pad_to(n)` fixture.
#
# **These are not another implementation's bytes, and saying so is the point.**
# Every other corpus here is laid out by somebody else's code -- glibc's
# `struct arphdr`, Python's `json`, an encoder that wrote a real PNG -- because
# a description agreeing only with its own compiler has demonstrated nothing.
# `padded.situ` describes no format anybody else implements: it exists so the
# four-backend differential draws buffers against exactly one construct
# (26.134). There is no second implementation to take bytes from.
#
# So these are computed by hand from section 8.4's rule, which is a second
# reading of the rule rather than a second implementation of it, and the
# weaker of the two kinds of evidence. What it does buy is the thing the skip
# named: `aligned_header` carries no `must_eq`, but 64 random draws produced
# no buffer the decoder accepted, so `test_owned` round-tripped nothing and
# said so. One vector it does accept exercises the decode and the re-encode.
#
# The arithmetic, stated so a reader can check it rather than trust it:
#
#   offset 0   one      u8     one byte
#   offset 1   <pad>    3 bytes, to the next multiple of 4
#   offset 4   word     u32    big-endian, per the file's `endian big`
#   ---------------------------------------------------------------
#              size(aligned_header) == 8
#
# which is what the schema's own `require size(aligned_header) == 8` and
# `require absolute_static(aligned_header.word)` already pin -- so a wrong
# layout here fails the build before it reaches this file. That is the half
# of the checking a hand-computed vector can lean on honestly.
#
# The pad is zeroed. Section 14.7 makes padding content explicit and `zero`
# what `require canonical` needs, and a re-encode has to reproduce the bytes
# it decoded for the round-trip to mean anything.

aligned_header word 2A 00 00 00 DE AD BE EF
